While working with the statuscolors patch (which can be found at
http://bilious.homelinux.org/?142), I discovered a problem that others
have also run into, judging from the comments; I posted the solution 
there, but am reposting it here in case people who have already tried
the patch and backed it out don't happen to check there.

The problem lies in one of the changes made to files.c; the
#ifdef/#endif section near line 1797 causes the assign_graphics() 
function to be called for every STATUSCOLORS= line, instead of 
the correct block (IBMGRAPHICS/DECGRAPHICS=).

The incorrect code, post-patching, reads as follows:

1797    } else if (match_varname(buf, "GRAPHICS", 4)) {
1798        len = get_uchars(fp, buf, bufp, translate, FALSE,
1799               MAXPCHARS, "GRAPHICS");
1800 #if defined(STATUS_COLORS) && defined(TEXTCOLOR)
1801    } else if (match_varname(buf, "STATUSCOLOR", 11)) {
1802        (void) parse_status_color_options(bufp);
1803 #endif
** 1804        assign_graphics(translate, len, MAXPCHARS, 0);

...and it should actually read as below.

1798    } else if (match_varname(buf, "GRAPHICS", 4)) {
1799        len = get_uchars(fp, buf, bufp, translate, FALSE,
1800               MAXPCHARS, "GRAPHICS");
** 1801        assign_graphics(translate, len, MAXPCHARS, 0);
1802 #if defined(STATUS_COLORS) && defined(TEXTCOLOR)
1803    } else if (match_varname(buf, "STATUSCOLOR", 11)) {
1804        (void) parse_status_color_options(bufp);
1805 #endif

